# Line endings, pinned.
#
# This is a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf on, so HEAD holds LF and the
# working tree holds CRLF. Files written by a tool running on Linux landed as
# LF, which git normalises before comparing and therefore does not report — but
# `git status` from a non-Windows shell then listed 253 modified files that
# were byte-identical in content, and the checkout was left with two line
# endings in one tree.
#
# `text=auto` makes it deterministic instead of dependent on whose machine
# wrote the file: LF in the repository, native on checkout.
* text=auto

# Always LF regardless of platform — these are read by tools, not editors, and
# a CRLF shebang line makes a script unexecutable on Unix.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

# Never touched.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip binary
